## Step 1 — Decompiling the DEX

I used [Androguard](https://github.com/androguard/androguard) to pull the app
source straight out of the DEX without needing an emulator or JADX GUI:

```python
from androguard.misc import AnalyzeAPK

a, d, dx = AnalyzeAPK("chal07.apk")
for c in d[0].get_classes():
    if "chal07" in c.get_name():
        print(c.get_source())
```

This surfaces two classes worth reading closely: `EnvCheck` and
`MainActivity`.

### `EnvCheck` — the JNI bridge

```java
public final class EnvCheck {
    public static final EnvCheck INSTANCE;

    static {
        INSTANCE = new EnvCheck();
        System.loadLibrary("check");
    }

    public final native String nativeDeriveFlag(
        String p0, double p1, long p2, long p3, long p4
    );
}
```

One native entry point, `nativeDeriveFlag`, taking a `String`, a `double`, and
three `long`s. Everything interesting happens on the native side.

### `MainActivity` — building the inputs

Trimmed to the relevant parts:

```java
private final void startSampling() {
    ...
    this.e0 = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();     // t0: register listener
    sensorManager.registerListener(this, accel, SensorManager.SENSOR_DELAY_NORMAL);
    handler.postDelayed(() -> { e1 = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime(); }, 1250);
    handler.postDelayed(() -> {
        e2 = SystemClock.elapsedRealtime();
        sensorManager.unregisterListener(this);
        finishCheck();
    }, 2500);
}

public void onSensorChanged(SensorEvent event) {
    float[] v = event.values;
    magnitudes.add(sqrt(v[0]*v[0] + v[1]*v[1] + v[2]*v[2]));
}

private final void finishCheck() {
    double mean     = magnitudes.average();
    double variance  = magnitudes.sumOf { (it - mean) * (it - mean) } / magnitudes.size();

    String fpHash = sha256Hex(Build.FINGERPRINT);

    String flag = EnvCheck.INSTANCE.nativeDeriveFlag(fpHash, variance, e0, e1, e2);
    status.setText(flag ?? "environment check failed");
}
```

So the five arguments handed to native code are exactly what the challenge
description promised:

| Arg | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `p0` (String) | `SHA-256(Build.FINGERPRINT)` in lowercase hex |
| `p1` (double) | Variance of accelerometer magnitude samples over ~2.5s |
| `p2` (long) | `e0` — timestamp when the sensor listener registered |
| `p3` (long) | `e1` — timestamp at the 1.25s mark |
| `p4` (long) | `e2` — timestamp at the 2.5s mark (listener unregistered) |

At this point the Kotlin/Java side is fully understood — all the actual
gatekeeping logic lives inside `libcheck.so`.

