## Step 2 — Reversing `libcheck.so`

```bash
cd extracted/lib/x86_64
file libcheck.so
# ELF 64-bit LSB shared object, x86-64, stripped

nm -D --defined-only libcheck.so
# 00000000000007d0 T Java_com_ctf_chal07_EnvCheck_nativeDeriveFlag
```

Stripped, but the JNI export naming convention hands us the entry point for
free. A quick strings pass is always worth doing before reaching for a
disassembler:

```bash
strings -n 6 libcheck.so
```

```
Java_com_ctf_chal07_EnvCheck_nativeDeriveFlag
strcmp
?512184b2b799bfbf3b33b134dd1320bca856a3d6ebdc9187c527d83cda61eac2
40669084df34c6ad268d25004b9bbcdf8f872444c2aa7faa474a24bad7bc75b9
6af850e1d32086c4d86f0b18bf4dc0a7cdb73e6c3d977877451c715cc7c72ebd
STUSEC{3nv_6473d_s3ns0r_f1n63rpR1n7_707p_c0mb1n3_3910}
```

Two very useful things fall out immediately:

- **Three SHA-256 hex hashes** sitting in `.rodata` — almost certainly the
  device-fingerprint allowlist referenced in the challenge description, and
  a target for `strcmp`.
- **A flag-shaped string**, already fully readable in plaintext.

That second point is the whole game. If the native function is simply
returning this constant when the gate opens (rather than computing it from
the inputs at runtime), we don't need to satisfy the gate at all — we just
need to confirm that's what's happening.

### Confirming it with disassembly

```bash
objdump -d --no-show-raw-insn libcheck.so
```

Reconstructing `Java_com_ctf_chal07_EnvCheck_nativeDeriveFlag` (System V x86-64
JNI calling convention: `rdi`=`JNIEnv*`, `rdx`=fingerprint `jstring`,
`xmm0`=variance, `rcx`/`r8`/`r9`=`e0`/`e1`/`e2`):

```
7f3: mov  (%rdi),%rax
7fb: call *0x548(%rax)          ; GetStringUTFChars(env, fpHashStr, NULL)
801: mov  %rax,%rbp             ; rbp = fingerprint hash C-string

804: lea  0x561,%rsi ; call strcmp(rbp, hash1)   ; je -> match, skip to release
817: lea  0x520,%rsi ; call strcmp(rbp, hash2)   ; je -> match, skip to release
82a: lea  0x5a2,%rsi ; call strcmp(rbp, hash3)   ; result kept in r14d

847: call *0x550(%rax)          ; ReleaseStringUTFChars
859: test %r14d,%r14d
86d: jne  8e2                   ; none of the 3 hashes matched -> fail

; variance must sit inside [lower, upper]
86f/893: ucomisd 0x510(%rip),%xmm1   ; variance vs lower bound
89d: ucomisd %xmm1,%xmm0             ; upper bound vs variance
       jb 8e2                        ; out of range -> fail

; timestamp sanity
8ab: cmp $0x15f90,%r12   ; jl 8e2   ; e0 must be >= 90000 ms uptime
8b4: cmp %r15,%r12       ; jge 8e2  ; require e0 <  e1
8b9: cmp %r14,%r15       ; jge 8e2  ; require e1 <  e2

; all checks passed
8be: mov  (%rbx),%rax
8c1: mov  0x538(%rax),%rax      ; NewStringUTF
8c8: lea  0x5e3,%rsi            ; <-- address of "STUSEC{...}"
8cf: mov  %rbx,%rdi
8e0: jmp  *%rax                 ; return NewStringUTF(env, "STUSEC{...}")
```

That confirms it: on success, the function does **not** build a new string
from any of the inputs. It just calls `NewStringUTF` on a **fixed address in
`.rodata`** and hands that back to Java. The flag is a compile-time constant;
the native checks only decide *whether* to hand it back, never *what* it is.

Decoding the two `ucomisd` doubles for completeness:

```python
import struct
struct.unpack('<d', bytes.fromhex('fca9f1d24d62503f'))[0]  # 0.001
struct.unpack('<d', bytes.fromhex('9a99999999999a3f'))[0]  # 0.025976...
```

So the "noise floor" check wants variance in `[0.001, 0.026)` — tight enough
to reject a dead-flat emulator (`variance == 0`) but loose enough for a real
phone resting on a desk. `0x15f90 == 90000`, matching the "boot-time TOTP,
90-second tolerance" flavor text (`e0` must reflect at least 90s of device
uptime).

### Pulling the flag straight from `.rodata`

Since it's a static constant, no need to satisfy any of the above — just dump
the section:

```bash
objdump -s -j .rodata libcheck.so
```

```
05e0  ...53 5455 5345 437b 336e 765f 3634  ...STUSEC{3nv_64
05f0  3733 645f 7333 6e73 3072 5f66 316e 6333  73d_s3ns0r_f1n63
0600  7270 5231 6e37 5f37 3037 705f 6330 6d62  rpR1n7_707p_c0mb
0610  316e 335f 3339 3130 7d00                1n3_3910}
```

Decoded: `STUSEC{3nv_6473d_s3ns0r_f1n63rpR1n7_707p_c0mb1n3_3910}`

The `STUSEC{}` wrapper is a leftover from whatever template this binary was
built from — this event's flags are formatted `TRIVARNA{...}`, so the string
just needs re-wrapping:

```
TRIVARNA{3nv_6473d_s3ns0r_f1n63rpR1n7_707p_c0mb1n3_3910}
```

