## Root Cause

The design intent was clearly "flag is unlocked only after passing an
environment/sensor/timing gate implemented in native code." The flaw is
architectural: **the value being protected and the logic protecting it live
in the same static, unencrypted binary.** The gate controls *whether*
`NewStringUTF` gets called on a fixed `.rodata` offset — it never transforms,
decrypts, or derives the string from the validated inputs. Since the checks
never actually change the bytes being returned, they add zero cost to an
attacker who reads the binary instead of running it.

A stronger version of this challenge would derive the flag as a function of
the validated inputs (e.g., XOR/AES-decrypt the flag using bytes drawn from
the fingerprint hash, the variance value, and the timestamps), so recovering
it requires actually satisfying — or precisely emulating — the runtime
checks, not just locating a string literal.

